A common cause of your JavaScript function returning undefined is that it's not properly returning a value. This can happen when the function is not returning a value at all, or when it's returning a value but not providing a way to access it.

For example, if your function is returning a string but not providing a way to access it, it will return undefined. If it's returning an object but not providing a way to access its properties, it will also return undefined.

Here's an example of how you might fix this:

```javascript
function myFunction() {
  return "Hello";
}

console.log(myFunction()); // returns undefined
```

If you're still getting undefined, it's possible that your function is not properly returning a value. You can check this by using the `console.log` statement to print the value of the function's return statement.

```javascript
function myFunction() {
  return "Hello";
}

console.log(myFunction()); // returns undefined
```

If you're still getting undefined, it's possible that your function is not properly returning a value. In this case, you can add a return statement to your function to provide a value.

```javascript
function myFunction() {
  return "Hello";
}

console.log(myFunction()); // returns "Hello"
```
